math_verify.metric:71 [[93mWARNING[0m] We did not manage to extract a prediction in the correct format. Gold: ['\\boxed{104}'], Pred: ["Okay, let me try to tackle this problem. So we have a tetrahedron ABCD with some specific edge lengths: AB=CD=√41, AC=BD=√80, and BC=AD=√89. There's a point I inside the tetrahedron such that its distances to each of the four faces are equal. We need to find this equal distance, expressed as m√n/p, and compute m + n + p.\n\nFirst, I need to recall some properties about tetrahedrons and points equidistant from all faces. If a point is equidistant from all the faces of a tetrahedron, it's likely the centroid or something related? But no, not exactly the centroid unless the tetrahedron is regular. Here, the tetrahedron isn't regular because all edges aren't the same length. Instead, this is a special symmetric tetrahedron perhaps. Wait, there are symmetries here.\n\nIt reminds me of something involving symmetric duals or maybe having congruences across opposite edges or maybe dual distances mirrored through their faces planes. The distances to opposite faces might be equidistant concepts—like equidistant geometry symmetry might imply that such tetrahedron lies balanced internally or exteriorly.\n\nLet us move forward focusing on the dual system called symmetry axis point mass idea itself (noting known) also geometrically balanced area weight sums equal distances perpendicular vectors geometry too.\n\nHowever, maybe better understand how tetrahedron itself balances surface area weights directly. For each interior volume formula applies surface triangle face normals vectors dot product respectively opposite triangle weights?\n\nLetting define sides lengths geometricly triangle-wise areas too known instead apply tetrahedron itself triangle sides faces area equalities? Hmm they've also triangle triangle weights geometry wrong area mass unit heights wrong instead trying angle relationships fixing wrong heights area themselves surface heights themselves.\n\nMaybe if angle relationships wrong area fixing surface area geometry side-wise wrong total weight mass balancing heights too?\n\nInstead define surface side heights equalization triangle areas directly balancing volume formulas themselves geometry height area height triangle triangle-wise triangle face triangle surface side triangle weights?\n\nWe suppose that total mass equalization occurs volume-wise center balancing mass balance triangle heights triangle sides themselves surface triangle height formula goes back into known heights heights equalization weights respectively mass balancing center heights side geometry goes into fixed weights triangle heights surface area mass unit equal triangle heights triangle sides surface height triangle fixed unit heights themselves triangle geometry surface heights triangle heights total heights vector mass surface height triangle itself balancing weights geometry vector heights heights themselves triangle unit fixing height fixed weights wrong height triangle side fixing heights side area unit respectively mass height area sides fixed balancing formula unit surface vector fixed wrong triangle side vector fixing heights wrong vector triangle heights surface unit balancing triangle heights formula heights themselves surface fixing okay fixed balancing heights sides heights area unit fixing heights triangle triangle vector height unit balancing triangle weights vector geometry fixed balancing heights sides heights unit heights themselves wrong balancing surface surface height fixing wrong vector geometry height fixing vector triangle unit heights sides balancing height itself formula wrong okay triangle itself surface triangle surface fixing geometry equal balancing triangle wrong triangle weights vector itself height triangle unit fixing weights height surface sides themselves triangle sides mass balancing height triangle heights unit fixed balancing triangle wrong fixed geometry triangle mass balancing weigh