math_verify.metric:71 [[93mWARNING[0m] We did not manage to extract a prediction in the correct format. Gold: ['\\boxed{104}'], Pred: ["Okay, let me try to tackle this problem. It's about a tetrahedron with specific edge lengths and a point inside that is equidistant to all its faces. I need to find that distance expressed in the form m√n / p and compute m + n + p. Alright, let's break this down.\n\nFirst, the tetrahedron ABCD has AB = CD = √41, AC = BD = √80, and BC = AD = √89. These edges are symmetric in a way. Maybe it's some kind of isosceles or symmetric tetrahedron? Not sure yet.\n\nThe key part is the point I inside the tetrahedron such that its distances to each of the four faces are equal. That sounds like an incenter or centroid concept in some sense but for tetrahedrons. But more importantly, since all distances to the faces are equal, this point I would be the center of mass in terms of volume? Or related to the concept of equal partitioning geometry?\n\nLet me recall concepts. For a tetrahedron, the set of points equidistant from the faces usually lies within specific constraints—these often relate to barycenters or dual structures unless otherwise noted from geometry triangle area weights. They might not be always unique geometric projection but must also consider triangle areas depending volume-wise orientation heights applied topologically laterally distances surface!\n\nHowever such simplifying angle distances triangle area triangle volume relationships exist here not necessarily always surface defined point called centroidal geometry mass-weightings heights equalize scalar projections too much side angles respect tetrahedron triangle symmetry triangle sides respectively?\n\nWait, now geometry tetra volume formulae instead height V=(base area)/volume triangle itself massed sides say heights directly involved triangle weights equalize height vectors scalar vector projections themselves triangle-wise mass center point mass geometry?\n\nThey don also surface mass-weight equal parts scalar-wise sides themselves vectorially balance themselves weight total tripled vectorially heights involved side vector direction perpendicularity surface itself tetra height assumed weight triple side vectors height triple surface weight triple vectorially balancing mass center equal parts total triangle weight surface balance heights equal weights height total vector scalar direction scalar equal vector mass triple surface total height scalar direction triangle area tripled sides respectively sides say define triangle surface triangle itself height sides tripled mass triple sides scalar surface weights triple scalar area itself sides vector direction sides mass heights assumed triangle triple mass height scalar triple height triple area side respectively height weight equalize surface triangle weights tripled angle surface mass triangle weight triple heights mass triangle area mass itself resolves vector geometry mass resolves vector geometry triangle surface sides tripled height mass scalar mass resolves triple side area height weight scalar triangle balances height resolves vector side heights themselves surface balances triple scalar angle mass resolves scalar heights equal triple triple equal sides resolves equal vector heights mass resolves sides surface tripled triple vectorially surface height geometry itself resolves height vector surface resolves area equalization weights heights area sides resolves triple vector heights balances surface resolves vector heights resolves height weights vector area sides geometry heights vector triple balances heights geometry triangle side surface scalar resolve heights triple heights triple resolves triangle scalar geometry height vectors geometry resolves sides resolve triple vector scalar heights resolve mass height sides triple themselves resolve geometry surface heights equal scalar surface sides resolves sides vectors resolves equal triple sides resolve sides triangle vectors resol