math_verify.metric:71 [[93mWARNING[0m] We did not manage to extract a prediction in the correct format. Gold: ['\\boxed{104}'], Pred: ["Okay, so I need to solve this problem about a tetrahedron ABCD with given edge lengths, and find a point I inside the tetrahedron that's equidistant from all faces. The distance can be expressed in a specific form, and then find m + n + p. Alright, let me try to break it down.\n\nFirst, the tetrahedron has AB = CD = √41, AC = BD = √80, and BC = AD = √89. That’s interesting. Maybe there's some symmetry or regularity here? I remember that in some symmetric tetrahedrons, you can find a center point equidistant from all faces. But how do I approach calculating that distance?\n\nHmm. For any polyhedron, if there's a point equidistant from all faces, it's called the incenter or centroid maybe? Wait no, in general polyhedra don't always have an incenter unless they are isohedral. Not sure if that applies here. But wait maybe properties might instead relate geometric volumes and areas. For tetrahedron especially, the distance from point inside to every face relates volume relations involving normals distances geometrically intersected out again via surface areas?\n\nLet define area faces too separately? Known triangle triangle sides instead area sides face calculations geometry later geometry instead surface triangle face triangle geometry surface sides again angle vectors? If yes suppose assume triangle area calculations must be done separately triangle formula such that total side vectors match mass balance geometry mass vector itself scale.\n\nActually here tetrahedrons geometry angle faces opposite vertices faces meet perpendicular angle sides not fixed triangle areas nor triangle triangle itself triangle scale unless known triangle vector sides placed mass surface vectors geometry face scale fixed angle respect opposite surface geometry vector scale.\n\nBut perhaps even triangle mass formula triangle triangle side opposite face opposite triangle area side geometry area known angle fixed face respect height surface formula scale geometry triangle height itself side triangle mass calculation vector sides opposite unknown mass vector respect angle respectively triangle triangle surface respect scale fixed?\n\nI suppose that total volume formulas triangle fixed tetrahedron volume height respect base surface height respectively equal fixed formula surface scale height respect volume unknown scale fixed unless they known wrong respect base formula itself respect scale mass height unknown mass equal area scale itself respect wrong triangle face area height base suppose formula triangle height = triangle respect scale formula unknown base scale triangle height = surface side unknown base fixed respect base triangle respect side fixed respect area triangle triangle scale respectively wrong height unknown triangle area formula base equal fixed height triangle height wrong area triangle sides angle fixed formula scale triangle mass unknown sides unknown wrong surface fixed formula geometry triangle area geometry sides scale height equal respect fixing surface side geometry wrong unknown surface mass triangle wrong sides unknown sides triangle triangle surface wrong surface suppose geometry sides area wrong heights scale wrong mass fixed equal triangle surface heights triangle triangle area respect heights triangle height surface fixed unknown triangle sides equal triangle surface angle height unknown suppose heights wrong triangle surface fixing angle triangle mass fixed triangle surface mass heights triangle triangle fixed triangle geometry side area fixed scale heights surface height unknown triangle sides triangle formula angle surface mass height fixing triangle surface suppose mass fixed wrong height sides respect heights scale equal height respect scale surface wrong triangle scale heights angle fixing respect height triangle height fixing mass triangle surface height area respect fixing formula wro